WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:05.080
This video will primarily be addressing the
first half of the Mandalorian season 3 and

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:06.980
I’ll also touch on episode 5.

00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:11.509
So far, this season feels hollow to me for
a multitude of different reasons and the first

00:00:11.509 --> 00:00:16.920
one I want to go into is the overall arc that
Mando has, or lack of one in season 3.

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:21.570
Through seasons 1 and 2 we go from a ruthless
bounty hunter who doesn’t ask questions,

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:26.180
to a more sympathetic father figure who cares
for this youngling he has bonded to.

00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:31.039
In this time, you could kind of excuse some
of the side quests for being fillery because

00:00:31.039 --> 00:00:35.320
at the bare minimum, Grogu’s and Mando’s
time together filled your interest.

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:40.050
It gave Mando a solid arc and it imbued him
with an immense purpose because he had an

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:41.940
important objective to achieve.

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:46.500
Any obstacle along the way was also pretty
fun because you had a lot of investment within

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:48.710
this duo in getting to their end goal.

00:00:48.710 --> 00:00:52.520
Like when Grogu gets kidnapped you immediately
feel that gut punch and the drive to get him

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:54.070
back is exciting.

00:00:54.070 --> 00:00:57.960
On top of the endpoint for this objective
being inherently interesting because we get

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:01.840
to meet a Jedi, along with there being a mystery
as to who it will be.

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:07.450
So from a plot basis and a character basis,
seasons 1 and 2 were able to keep you captivated.

00:01:07.450 --> 00:01:11.979
In season 3, there hasn’t been a strong
and engaging throughline to hook you as a

00:01:11.979 --> 00:01:12.979
viewer.

00:01:12.979 --> 00:01:16.750
Especially for the first 4 episodes where
the plot meanders a lot with not much to look

00:01:16.750 --> 00:01:18.030
forward to.

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:22.020
Mando doesn’t really have any semblance
of an arc so far and his only objective was

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:24.330
to bathe in the waters of Mandalore.

00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:28.619
Which in turn, this goal didn’t really give
him much to work with as a character since

00:01:28.619 --> 00:01:32.299
he’s just fulfilling some arbitrary objective
to redeem himself.

00:01:32.299 --> 00:01:37.049
Once he achieves this goal, the show doesn’t
establish another objective for Mando.

00:01:37.049 --> 00:01:41.090
However, episode 5 does a good job at partially
rectifying this issue.

00:01:41.090 --> 00:01:45.140
Giving Mando the task of helping Nevarro was
pretty good and I’m interested in seeing

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:47.550
how this development evolves later on.

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:50.960
Even though it feels like the writers are
starting to set a precedence in that they

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:54.330
aren’t interested in giving Mando much character
development.

00:01:54.330 --> 00:01:58.260
It seems like he’s hit a point where he’s
stayed the same since the end of season 2.

00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:02.509
I wish they would explore him learning how
to use the dark saber more because that’s

00:02:02.509 --> 00:02:07.890
by far the coolest aspect the show has set
up, but yet, it feels largely neglected.

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:12.420
The creators seem to be shifting their focus
towards Bo Katan which is fine to a degree,

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:15.000
but this in turn leaves Mando out to dry.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:19.569
Now you may be thinking that Mando doesn’t
need much deep character work which is partially

00:02:19.569 --> 00:02:20.569
true.

00:02:20.569 --> 00:02:24.640
However, what made the earlier seasons so
compelling was the bond between Mando and

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:27.810
Grogu and the amount of heart that came with
that.

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:31.900
So since that arc has been finished, there
is a big void at the core of The Mandalorian

00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:34.840
that needs to be filled, and that hasn’t
in my opinion.

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:39.460
I think they tried to refill that void by
bringing back Grogu, but it feels very forced

00:02:39.460 --> 00:02:43.310
and it’s as if Disney panicked and brought
him back as soon as possible.

00:02:43.310 --> 00:02:47.190
It seems like they didn’t really understand
what made him so special within the story

00:02:47.190 --> 00:02:50.360
and now he’s mostly here to be cute and
play paintball.

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:53.790
To me, It would make the most sense that the
original plan for the show was to have Grogu

00:02:53.790 --> 00:02:59.300
exit permanently after season 2, or at worst
coming back after season 3.

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:03.420
Because by bringing him back so soon, it diminishes
that strong farewell.

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:06.690
Which was by far the highest point in the
show and I don’t know if they’ll ever

00:03:06.690 --> 00:03:09.549
be able to get to that level of quality again.

00:03:09.549 --> 00:03:13.330
To make things even worse, they included Grogu’s
return in The Book of Bobba Fett which was

00:03:13.330 --> 00:03:15.209
just a wild decision.

00:03:15.209 --> 00:03:19.970
Having such a huge development for Mando not
in his own show is baffling to me.

00:03:19.970 --> 00:03:23.530
Especially since those two episodes of The
Mandalorian within The Book of Boba Fett were

00:03:23.530 --> 00:03:27.050
far meatier than what we’ve gotten in most
of season 3 so far.

00:03:27.050 --> 00:03:31.409
So I feel bad for the casual viewers who were
confused when Grogu was back because they

00:03:31.409 --> 00:03:33.030
didn’t even explain it in the recap.

00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:37.260
But yeah, Grogu’s place in the show feels
artificial, and seems like he’s just there

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:40.000
because Disney didn’t want to take a hit
in merchandise sales.

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:44.530
I can’t shake the feeling that the original
plan for season 3 was a Bo Katan and Mando

00:03:44.530 --> 00:03:46.290
arc without Grogu.

00:03:46.290 --> 00:03:50.000
I think it would have been much better if
they solely paired these two characters together

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:53.400
because what’s happening to Bo right now
is kind of interesting.

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:58.040
Albeit it’s pretty underdeveloped so far
considering how short the episodes have been.

00:03:58.040 --> 00:04:02.329
The writers really need to write more dialogue
scenes and commit to actual character development

00:04:02.329 --> 00:04:06.599
because the little screen time we’ve had
is mostly reserved for action.

00:04:06.599 --> 00:04:10.230
Which is fun, but you can only get so far
with spectacle.

00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:14.579
On top of also spending a ton of time exploring
characters like Dr. Pershing.

00:04:14.579 --> 00:04:18.359
The fact that he has gotten significantly
more character work in comparison to our main

00:04:18.359 --> 00:04:21.430
hero is insane from a writing perspective.

00:04:21.430 --> 00:04:25.800
The episodes are essentially averaging 30ish
minutes and with an additional 10 minutes

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:29.450
of time focusing on characters an episode,
it would feel a lot better.

00:04:29.450 --> 00:04:33.482
That’s what the standard should be for these
kinds of shows especially since there are

00:04:33.482 --> 00:04:38.800
only eight episodes.This is about the same
amount of content in the past, but since we

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:43.250
are lacking certain elements like solid objectives
and character work, along with other things

00:04:43.250 --> 00:04:47.300
I’ll bring up later in the video, it feels
like we’re receiving less value from each

00:04:47.300 --> 00:04:48.300
episode.

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:51.759
Because of this lack of care being put into
the script lengths, I’m feeling more and

00:04:51.759 --> 00:04:54.600
more detached from Mando and it feels like
he’s fading away.

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:58.669
I also hate how they are kind of knocking
down his character to have Bo repeatedly save

00:04:58.669 --> 00:05:00.419
him like in episode 2.

00:05:00.419 --> 00:05:05.180
Him blindly walking into an aquifer while
also taking off his jetpack was really silly

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:06.180
to me.

00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:10.040
I think there can definitely be a better execution
for their pairing in which you’d treat them

00:05:10.040 --> 00:05:11.699
more as equals.

00:05:11.699 --> 00:05:16.940
Or at the very least, have Mando actively
progress to a point where he matches Bo’s

00:05:16.940 --> 00:05:17.940
level.

00:05:17.940 --> 00:05:21.350
Along with giving him a complimentary arc
so that he’s not aimlessly following her

00:05:21.350 --> 00:05:22.350
around.

00:05:22.350 --> 00:05:27.100
There may just be eight episodes, but this
is something that is necessary for storytelling.

00:05:27.100 --> 00:05:31.290
Another remedy would be to reduce Grogu’s
time in the show and allocate that time to

00:05:31.290 --> 00:05:32.690
other characters.

00:05:32.690 --> 00:05:36.850
A lot of his moments are starting to feel
more like fan service instead of progressing

00:05:36.850 --> 00:05:39.180
any form of relationship or the story.

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:41.580
An example being the paintball scene.

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:45.600
Unless if they are developing Grogu into becoming
a Mandalorian or something, which would just

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:49.979
be ridiculous, then him training here is not
necessary for the plot.

00:05:49.979 --> 00:05:53.940
Cutting out Grogu in general isn’t currently
attainable since he’s already back, but

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:57.050
they really need some more scenes between
Mando and Bo.

00:05:57.050 --> 00:06:00.819
And dialogue that isn’t solely driven by
lore like in episode 2.

00:06:00.819 --> 00:06:03.699
Because it felt like Bo was a tour guide in
that episode.

00:06:03.699 --> 00:06:08.040
Also by leaving Grogu out of the season, it
shakes up the status quo of the show and it

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:12.040
forces the writers to get creative, and not
rely on Grogu’s cuteness.

00:06:12.040 --> 00:06:16.360
And the thing is that the setting for most
of the season is within this group of Mandalorians

00:06:16.360 --> 00:06:21.169
so the recipe is already shaken up and the
writers have to utilize that fact more.

00:06:21.169 --> 00:06:25.760
I’d much rather have scenes of the Mandalorians
telling stories and talking to each other,

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:29.349
rather than Grogu having more PTSD flashbacks.

00:06:29.349 --> 00:06:33.840
Or hell, even establishing more Mandalorian
characters because it’s a pretty sparse

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:35.250
roster right now.

00:06:35.250 --> 00:06:39.000
All we have is the armorer and this big dude
and that’s it.

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:42.870
If we had a few other characters to look to
within the ranks of the Mandalorians, this

00:06:42.870 --> 00:06:47.000
group would be much more interesting and you
could spawn more dialogue scenes naturally

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:48.790
thus growing your characters.

00:06:48.790 --> 00:06:53.050
I feel like a golden opportunity to add in
a scene with a bit of character development

00:06:53.050 --> 00:06:55.930
would have been during the dinner by the fire
in episode 4.

00:06:55.930 --> 00:07:00.160
They could have easily included some dialogue
before they started eating and instead, the

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:03.940
writers end it quickly by passing out food
and everyone leaves.

00:07:03.940 --> 00:07:08.889
The thing that stings even more is that this
episode is only 26 minutes long and if you

00:07:08.889 --> 00:07:13.289
cut out the Grogu flashback which felt redundant,
given that we learned about that in season

00:07:13.289 --> 00:07:17.090
2 and by seeing all of it, it doesn’t add
much to the story, the episode would have

00:07:17.090 --> 00:07:19.050
been 22 minutes long.

00:07:19.050 --> 00:07:23.450
So it feels like Jon Favreau, whose the sole
writer of season 3 so far, is not putting

00:07:23.450 --> 00:07:25.620
enough time and care into the scripts.

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:30.349
And he’s been the primary writer for both
The Mandalorian and The Book of Boba Fett,

00:07:30.349 --> 00:07:34.220
so I’m starting to think that he’s the
one that’s probably at fault.

00:07:34.220 --> 00:07:38.190
If there’s anyone to blame for the botchery
of The Book of Boba Fett, it would be Jon

00:07:38.190 --> 00:07:40.540
Favearu because those scripts were dogshit.

00:07:40.540 --> 00:07:42.949
I’m sorry but they were.

00:07:42.949 --> 00:07:47.250
The Mandalorian season 3 is starting to show
its cracks and the lack of expansion overall

00:07:47.250 --> 00:07:50.210
and evolution of Mando is hindering the show.

00:07:50.210 --> 00:07:54.580
Because the fact that we are 5 episodes in
and we had a beginning episode that was absolutely

00:07:54.580 --> 00:07:58.940
pointless given that Mando just gets some
other random droid instead of the one he wanted

00:07:58.940 --> 00:08:03.419
on Nevarro, episode 3 primarily following
characters who aren’t relevant to the current

00:08:03.419 --> 00:08:08.449
plot, and episode 4 which is 22 minutes without
the flashback is starting to show some worrying

00:08:08.449 --> 00:08:10.319
signs for the writing.

00:08:10.319 --> 00:08:14.289
Episode 5 is giving me some hope, but I’m
afraid that this sudden burst of productivity

00:08:14.289 --> 00:08:18.720
in the plot will be met with 2 episodes where
barely anything happens.

00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:23.159
When other shows like The Last of Us have
more character and plot development in a single

00:08:23.159 --> 00:08:27.580
episode than half of your season then you
need to provide more content.

00:08:27.580 --> 00:08:31.780
Not to mention the fact that there’s been
a 3-year time gap between seasons 2 and 3.

00:08:31.780 --> 00:08:35.080
It’s crazy to me that season 2 was back
in 2020.

00:08:35.080 --> 00:08:36.719
We’re getting old man.

00:08:36.719 --> 00:08:39.830
So the amount of content and quality that
we are getting feels rough.

00:08:39.830 --> 00:08:44.830
I know we’ve gotten lots of side quest episodes
in the past, but at the very least they were

00:08:44.830 --> 00:08:47.010
motivated by a general objective.

00:08:47.010 --> 00:08:51.010
And there was even an overarching villain
presence within the first two seasons.

00:08:51.010 --> 00:08:55.040
Moff Gideon was a menacing character who was
always operating in the background to apply

00:08:55.040 --> 00:08:56.700
pressure to our characters.

00:08:56.700 --> 00:09:01.250
And the stormtroopers that Mando occasionally
ran into, felt like an extension of Moff Gideon’s

00:09:01.250 --> 00:09:02.250
power.

00:09:02.250 --> 00:09:06.529
In season 3, that villain threat is Gorian
Shard and it’s obviously a major step down,

00:09:06.529 --> 00:09:08.279
but he is a short-term villain.

00:09:08.279 --> 00:09:11.730
The biggest issue with Gorian is that his
presence at this point in the show relies

00:09:11.730 --> 00:09:13.420
on a major contrivance.

00:09:13.420 --> 00:09:18.399
Firstly, him having a form of peace with Nevarro
is kind of weird because pirates aren’t

00:09:18.399 --> 00:09:20.190
known for making peace.

00:09:20.190 --> 00:09:22.980
They do whatever they want and raid whatever
they want.

00:09:22.980 --> 00:09:28.140
Then secondly, the fact that Greef Karga has
no defenses on Nevarro and has the audacity

00:09:28.140 --> 00:09:30.420
to say this: “Sure you want to let him go?

00:09:30.420 --> 00:09:35.720
He’ll let it be known that Nevarro is respectable
and not to be trifled with.”

00:09:35.720 --> 00:09:36.720
Like what?

00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:39.230
You literally just gunned down a bunch of
pirates which will undoubtedly antagonize

00:09:39.230 --> 00:09:40.230
their leader.

00:09:40.230 --> 00:09:43.339
And what the hell is stopping him from raiding
Nevarro now?

00:09:43.339 --> 00:09:47.101
There are zero defenses and Greef Karga I
guess just hopes that Gorian won’t attack

00:09:47.101 --> 00:09:49.640
his city because nothing is stopping him.

00:09:49.640 --> 00:09:53.890
It’s also funny how Greef only sends out
a notice for help until they get attacked

00:09:53.890 --> 00:09:57.690
and doesn’t bother with any safety precautions
for if Gorian does attack.

00:09:57.690 --> 00:10:01.730
It’s just a very stupid scenario and is
only this way so that Mando can get the help

00:10:01.730 --> 00:10:04.110
of the Mandalorians to save the day.

00:10:04.110 --> 00:10:08.380
So this brief villain was not great, and I
feel like people are loving this so much right

00:10:08.380 --> 00:10:11.240
now because we are finally getting some form
of progression.

00:10:11.240 --> 00:10:15.660
Despite this being the fifth episode in the
season and there are only 3 left now.

00:10:15.660 --> 00:10:19.161
Hopefully, Jon Favreau will start applying
some pressure towards our heroes since they

00:10:19.161 --> 00:10:21.810
finally announced that Moff Gideon escaped.

00:10:21.810 --> 00:10:25.600
At this point, if any character doesn’t
die on-screen in Star Wars they are guaranteed

00:10:25.600 --> 00:10:30.180
to come back, or even if they do die then
they have a high chance of coming back too.

00:10:30.180 --> 00:10:34.310
But hopefully, Moff’s return will spawn
a more consistent throughline of conflict

00:10:34.310 --> 00:10:36.860
that will make future obstacles more engaging.

00:10:36.860 --> 00:10:41.279
If I had to guess, they’ll probably reserve
his appearance for a finale cliffhanger and

00:10:41.279 --> 00:10:44.190
will slow down the pace for the next couple
of episodes.

00:10:44.190 --> 00:10:45.899
I do hope I’m wrong though.

00:10:45.899 --> 00:10:50.040
Overall, most of the season has a lack of
purpose and objective, development for our

00:10:50.040 --> 00:10:55.130
main character feels non-existent, the villain
presence is lacking, and the return of Grogu

00:10:55.130 --> 00:10:57.350
is cheap and detracts from the story at this
point.

00:10:57.350 --> 00:11:01.820
I know there are still 3 episodes left and
the show can turn around so I’m reserving

00:11:01.820 --> 00:11:05.279
my final judgment for an overall review when
the season ends.

00:11:05.279 --> 00:11:09.760
As a side note, I’m on the fence about continuing
the Baby Yoda But with Subtitles series.

00:11:09.760 --> 00:11:13.610
I feel like I should since they are really
popular, but it feels weird because I haven’t

00:11:13.610 --> 00:11:16.190
made those videos in a while, so we’ll see.

00:11:16.190 --> 00:11:19.920
Regardless though, thank you for watching,
and let me know in the comments what you think

00:11:19.920 --> 00:11:22.170
about The Mandalorian season 3 so far.

